course: dictionary
lesson: 9633
title: One word, many meanings: stuff
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Put your stuff in your bag.» Here stuff means things in general, when you do not need to name each one. Your pencil case, your book and your snack are all stuff, so you do not have to list them.
explain: Stuff can also mean a material or substance. «What is this sticky stuff?» asks what something is made of, like glue or jam. The clues in the sentence show which meaning fits.
explain: Stuff can be a verb too. «Stuff a pillow» means fill it tightly. «He stuffed the letter into his pocket» means pushed it quickly into a small space. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Stuff is an informal word. In your own writing, try to use more exact words instead of stuff and things. Instead of «I packed my stuff», you could write «I packed my books, my pencils and my lunch».
fact: Stuff can be a noun (things, or a material) or a verb (to fill something, or to push something).
fact: «Stuff» meaning things: «Put your stuff in your bag.»
fact: «Stuff» meaning a material: «What is this sticky stuff?» A material is a substance, like glue or jam.
fact: «Stuff» meaning to fill tightly: «A hamster can stuff its cheeks so full of seeds» that its head looks wider.
fact: «I'm stuffed» is an informal phrase meaning I have eaten too much.
exercise: «Put your stuff in your bag.» What does stuff mean here? | *Things in general | To fill something tightly | To push something quickly | A material, like glue
exercise: «A hamster can stuff its cheeks so full of seeds.» What does stuff mean here? | *To fill something tightly | Things in general | To push something quickly into a small space | A material or substance
exercise: «He stuffs it in his bag.» Which meaning of stuff fits this sentence? | Things in general | To fill something tightly | *To push something quickly into a small space, like a note pushed into a bag | To push something quickly into a small space
test: «What is this sticky stuff?» What does stuff mean here? | *A material or substance | To push something quickly | To fill something tightly | Things in general
test: Ellie says, «I'm stuffed after that roast dinner.» What does she mean? | She is filling a pillow | She is packing her bag | She has lost all her things | *She has eaten too much
exam: Which sentence uses stuff to mean fill something tightly? | *Stuff the pillow with soft feathers. | I'm stuffed. | What is this sticky stuff? | Put your stuff in your bag.
